DC2-PROGRAM.md-28-six CCRs
DC2-PROGRAM.md-29-  [Q_i, P_j] = δ_ij,  [P_1, P_2] = 0,  [Q_1, Q_2] = 0,
DC2-PROGRAM.md-30-(P_j = φ(x_j), Q_i = φ(∂_i)). A DC(2)-counterexample = such a quadruple whose generated
DC2-PROGRAM.md-31-subalgebra is **proper**.
DC2-PROGRAM.md-32-
DC2-PROGRAM.md-33-Caveat on the "pair" phrasing: in A_1, DC(1) ⟺ every pair [Q,P]=1 generates. In A_2 the
DC2-PROGRAM.md-34-pair version is *trivially false and irrelevant*: (P,Q) = (x_1, ∂_1) has [Q,P]=1 and
DC2-PROGRAM.md-35-generates a proper subalgebra. The one-pair intuition survives only as: each (Q_i, P_i) is
DC2-PROGRAM.md:36:a Dixmier pair *inside the centralizer of the other two generators*, and DC(1)-type
DC2-PROGRAM.md-37-rigidity is what one would like to apply there (see §2 for why one can't, yet).
