xmodel/sol-dixmier.log-1052-xmodel/codex-eval-20260812.md:2794:**A. Residue-A is treated as an object that “must die.”**  
xmodel/sol-dixmier.log:1053:xmodel/codex-eval-20260812.md:2803:Claim in the current narrative: residue-A is “what stands between the campaign and \(\mathrm{td}\le 14\) multi-pole exclusion.”  
xmodel/sol-dixmier.log-1054-xmodel/codex-eval-20260812.md:2804:Why fragile: even a complete kill of all 27 multi-pole instances leaves \(\mathrm{td}=6\) open on the four thesis-forced single-pole classes (entry \(Q=(3,6,5,2,8)\), type \((3,5)\), \(M=b=2\)), and every composite \(\mathrm{td}\) independently blocked by TDU. Those four have not been hit with the T1 Wronskian ladder that just killed 15 multi-pole classes. “Multi-pole book drained” \(\neq\) “\(\mathrm{td}\) excluded.”
xmodel/sol-dixmier.log-1055-xmodel/codex-eval-20260812.md:2814:| 1 | **T1 ladder on the 4 r9/\(M=2\) single-pole classes** | Same closed-form weapon that killed ZCH iff \((\nu+1)\mid l\) and family I iff \(l/r\) or \(r/l\in\mathbb{Z}\). Hours, exact. A hit is the first new single-pole kill since TDU and may uniformize composite \(\mathrm{td}\). | At the first non-pole \(V_a\) vertex of entry \(Q=(3,6,5,2,8)\), write Prop 8.1(iv) and run the triangular coefficient ladder (`l1_ode_check` style). Kill = \(\tilde{c}=0\) or a multiple root. |
xmodel/sol-dixmier.log:1056:xmodel/codex-eval-20260812.md:2815:| 2 | **Hurwitz factorization of \(\hat g:\overline{C}\to\mathbb{P}^1\) in \(S_6\)** | Classical battery used RH as a *number*. Existence of an actual degree-6 cover with the pinned local types is a different obstruction; failure kills residue-A independently of Sigray. | From Newton pairs \((7,2)(3,10)(2,5)\) at each pole, compute the local monodromy cycle type on the 6 sheets of \(\hat g\) (poles of order 3). Check whether two such classes, plus the \(x\)-side cluster (\(g\) finite there) and RH-constrained finite ramification, multiply to \(1\) in \(S_6\). |
xmodel/sol-dixmier.log-1057-xmodel/codex-eval-20260812.md:2817:| 4 | **Theorem A on the depth-3 column of the two-pole support** | Connects the two successful lanes, which have been run in parallel. MATHIEU-REVIEW already has the depth-3 column \(2BE'-(k+1)B'E\). Residue-A is *not* a strip; if that column cannot vanish for the pinned leading forms, the genome is not a polynomial pair. | Take the Newton edges \((126,42)\), \((189,63)\) with root pattern \(p_R=S_R(\eta-c_0)^{126}\); extract the \(x^{k+2}\) bracket column; run the leading-coefficient lemma (MATHIEU §5.1 Step 1). |
xmodel/sol-dixmier.log:1058:xmodel/codex-eval-20260812.md:2818:| 5 | **Mixed-merge children of residue-A (not of off-axis entries)** | BOOK-ENUM’s “\(M\ge 2\) chain engine” is about \(b_i\ge 2\) *entries*. The live hole is a *second* merge with all \(\mu_e\ge 2\) downstream of the \(M=2\) jump, legal at \(m\ge 3\). Either the book is missing cells, or the quarantine closes. | From child \(Q=(6,12,3,2,5)\), enumerate MP6(b)/(d) mixed merges with \(\mu_e\ge 2\), \(r\le 4\), \(\mathrm{td}\le 14\). Any new \((p,q)\) not among the 27 survivors cracks completeness. |
xmodel/sol-dixmier.log-1059-xmodel/codex-eval-20260812.md:2824:Dim-2 keeps proving the statements that are true (GMC(2), Shaska for every weight sign, no graded plane CE). Every known \(n\ge 3\) mechanism dies in the plane (Picard/\(d=3\) needle, no tangent-sweep, no graded escape). Eighty-five years without an example is evidence. Residue-A is the best formal candidate I have seen in this literature, but “passes the printed list” is exactly Theorem O, and an étale non-proper degree-6 endomorphism of \(\mathbb{A}^2\) is the thing JC2 says does not exist — consistency at vertices is cheap compared with algebraizing 126 series.
