    40	(St 8.1) at H, transport of (k,l) to the root, k = 1, l = l_f/k_f in N,
    41	contra Thm 6.1 (l_f < k_f). The singleton hypothesis is NEVER mentioned in
    42	the proof body. It enters exactly once, silently, through Prop 8.3's
    43	hypothesis (p. 44, verbatim): "Assume that for any H in Ta& cap (Va\{(0,y)})
    44	one has G != H°" — as printed this contradicts G := F° (take H = F); the
    45	coherent reading (= Notation 9.2's "G regular over F", p. 48) is: F is the
    46	UNIQUE Ta&-Va-predecessor of G. The induction needs regularity at EVERY step.
    47	Why singleton-pole => regularity: Prop 6.8 (p. 34, verbatim): every
    48	F in Ta& with deg(p_F) != 1 lies under a pole vertex ("there exist P, v >= u
    49	with I_P(v) in T_{a,pole}"). If some chain vertex G had two distinct
    50	predecessors H != H' in Va cap Ta& (deg p >= 2 each, St 3.16), the two
    51	disjoint subtrees above G would each contain a pole vertex (Prop 6.8), so
    52	|T_{a,pole}| >= 2. Contrapositive: one pole => every ° step of every chain is
    53	regular => Prop 8.3 applies inductively => Prop 8.4. Secondary uses of
    54	regularity in the same proof: Prop 8.3(ii) gives the single-orbit root
    55	pattern p_G = ⊖(η^ν−c^ν)^M, and 8.3(iii) gives p_{(0,y)} = (η−c)^k
    56	(one root), which is what makes deg = mult at (0,y) in the k-computation.
