    58	### 1c. What survives with two poles: the merge trichotomy
    59	
    60	Let T_{a,pole} = {P1, P2} (both row 1, §2). Both characteristic sequences
    61	(Prop 9.2) are °-descents ending at (0,y); ° is deterministic, so they
    62	coalesce at a unique meet G_m and share the suffix from G_m to (0,y).
    63	Structure facts (all from printed statements):
    64	- (i) G_m is NOT a pole vertex: Prop 5.3(v) (p. 25) makes p squarefree at
    65	  pole vertices, while the incoming chain orbit has mult(p_{G_m}, c_i) =
    66	  deg(p_{H_i}) >= 2 (St 3.17(i), p. 18; H_i in Va so deg p_{H_i} >= 2 by
    67	  St 3.16). So each pre-merge segment has >= 1 edge (H_i = P_i allowed).
    68	- (ii) Regularity fails EXACTLY at G_m (over both H_1, H_2) and nowhere
    69	  else: a second regularity failure would manufacture a third pole via
    70	  Prop 6.8. Two sub-cases: interior merge (G_m != (0,y), G_m in V_{2,a})
    71	  or root merge (G_m = (0,y); then p_{(0,y)} has >= 2 roots, so (0,y) in
    72	  V_{2,a} — (0,y) in V_{1,a} is impossible — and BOTH terminal steps are
    73	  Prop 9.3 case (I), never case (IV): the case-IV hypothesis
    74	  "F not in V_{1,a} cup V_{2,a}" fails).
    75	- (iii) M=1 kill PARTIALLY RESTORED: for F on {G_m} cup (suffix), the
    76	  descent from F is regular at every step and (0,y) is regular over the
    77	  shared H := F_{n-1} (a second predecessor of any suffix vertex would be a
    78	  third pole), so Prop 8.3's induction + the p. 45 Bezout run verbatim:
    79	  M_F = 1 is contradictory ON THE SHARED SUFFIX AND AT G_m (interior case).
    80	  Above G_m (pre-merge segments) the propagation stops at the non-regular
    81	  step: M = 1 is NOT killable there. In the root-merge case 8.3(iii) fails
    82	  at the last step (p_{(0,y)} has two roots, deg != mult), so no M=1 kill
    83	  anywhere.
