   188	## 4. The two-pole chain calculus (new lemma content)
   189	
   190	Conventions as in the campaign engine (reduced patterns p,q of Prop 8.1;
   191	child F = G°, parent G = F + c; St 8.2 (p. 41) is the searrow test at a
   192	root c: G' := F + c in Ta& iff deg(q)·mult(p,c) > deg(p), northeast iff <).
   193	
   194	### 4a. Merge-step patterns (hypothesis M-PAT, the H1/H4-extension)
   195	
   196	At F = G_m both incoming directions are searrow, so p has TWO orbits
   197	passing the St 8.2 test, plus k northeast extras (λ-charged via St 9.3 as
   198	in AF2), i.e. per branch shape (ν := ν_F):
   199	    IIa-like: dp = (μ_1+μ_2+k)ν,   dq = (k+2)ν + 1
   200	    I-like  : dp = μ_1+μ_2+k,      dq = k+2          (ν = 1)
   201	    IIb-like: dp = (μ_1+μ_2+k)ν+1, dq = (k+2)ν + 1
   202	    III-like: dp = μ_1+μ_2+kν,     dq = 2 + kν
   203	with each searrow orbit once in q (same per-root rule that puts the chain
   204	orbit once in q in the thesis's own patterns — this extension of the
   205	single-pole pattern zoo is flagged M-PAT; same trust tier as H1/H4).
   206	Two ratio equations must hold simultaneously (one per edge, Prop 9.3(b)):
   207	    dp/dq = μ_1(ρ_1+n_1)/(κ̄_1+n_1) = μ_2(ρ_2+n_2)/(κ̄_2+n_2),
   208	n_i >= 1, n_i ≡ −κ̄_i mod ν_i, plus the §2b.2 consistency (same κ̄, D, i).
   209	λ(merge) = k·max(1, ceil(D/i − κ̄)) — the OTHER chain's orbit is never
   210	λ-charged (it is searrow: no cv vertex hangs there, St 9.3 needs
   211	F + c in Ta%). The single-pole engine has no such step: its k extras are
   212	all northeast and all charged. Searrow-orbit multiplicity floor: μ_i ≥ 1
   213	with i·μ_i = deg p_{H_i} (full), and μ_i > dp/dq whenever dp > dq.
   214	
   215	### 4b. Kill set for the two-pole BFS
   216	
   217	- Pre-merge segments: NO M=1 kill (§1c(iii)); all other campaign kills
   218	  (budget, no-applicable-case, loop closure) stand.
   219	- Merge child: M(G_m) = gcd(dp, dq) (Prop 8.1(v)); if = 1 the §1c(iii)
   220	  restored kill fires (interior merge) — KILLED.
   221	- Suffix: full single-pole kill set (M=1, budget, H3q IV-dispositions of
   222	  hiii_compose/h3_check) with the λ already spent by both pre-merge
   223	  segments + merge.
   224	- Root merge: terminal step is the I-like merge pattern with child
   225	  (ν,κ̄,D) = (1, 1, l_f) (St 9.2), so κ̄_i + n_i = ν_i, i.e. BOTH parents
   226	  need κ̄ < ν (the case-IV condition (j) reappears); ψ = ceil(k_f/l_f)−1
   227	  with k_f = i(μ_1+μ_2+k)·[+ other roots], l_f = D_root.
   228	
   229	### 4c. Two rigidity facts inside the calculus (exact)
   230	
   231	1. μ = 1 chains are λ-free but IIa_0-only: with μ = 1 the searrow test
   232	   for the chain orbit reads dq > dp, while a northeast extra needs
   233	   dq < dp — so k = 0 and the only admissible shapes are IIa_0-like
   234	   (dp = ν, dq = (l+1)ν+1), λ = 0. (I/IIb/III-like shapes force
   235	   dp/dq = 1 = ratio, impossible since ρ < κ̄.) Consequence: M=1 ancestry
   236	   (μ=1 forced, §2c) descends λ-free — the budget CANNOT kill M=1
   237	   pre-merge segments; only the merge-time M-kill (§4b) can.
   238	2. Root-merge ratio rigidity at μ_1 = μ_2 = 1: the I-like merged ratio is
   239	   (2+k)/(k+2) = 1 — impossible. So two M=1 chains can never terminate
   240	   together at (0,y); an M=1 chain must merge INTO an interior vertex.
   241	   Root merges need μ_1 + μ_2 + k > k + 2, i.e. some μ_i >= 2, i.e. at
   242	   least one chain arrives with M >= 2 at a κ̄ < ν vertex.
