    88	## 2. DS1 (characteristic rigidity): every segment vertex is a V_{1,a} characteristic vertex of its own pole, and every chain edge is Prop 9.3 case (II)
    89	
    90	**Claim.** For 1 ≤ j ≤ d: (a) ν_j ≥ 2 and the orbit base c ≠ 0; (b)
    91	F_j ∈ V_{1,a}; (c) κ̄_{F_j} ∈ ℤ; (d) π(F_j) is a characteristic exponent of
    92	P_i's own Puiseux series, the segment vertices are *consecutive*
    93	characteristic vertices I_{P_i}(α_s), and each edge F_{j-1} → F_j is case
    94	(II) of Prop 9.3 (p. 50), so its (a)–(d) equations hold verbatim.
    95	
    96	*Proof.* (a) F_j ∈ V_a ∩ Ta& \ {(0,x),(0,y)} (MP0), and St 3.16 (p. 17) is
    97	an iff: p_{F_j} has more than one root. The single simple ν-orbit has root
    98	set {ζc : ζ^ν = 1} — more than one root forces ν ≥ 2 and c ≠ 0. (This is
    99	the thesis's own step: St 9.6's proof, p. 52, "by Statements 3.16 and 3.18
   100	one has p(η) = ⊖(η^ν − c^ν) for some ν = ν_F".)
   101	
   102	(b) Not 3.4 (p. 12): ν_F := 1 unless F ∈ V_{1,a}; so ν_j ≥ 2 ⟹ F_j ∈
   103	V_{1,a}, i.e. F_j = I_{P′}(α′_{j′}) at a characteristic value of some
   104	P′ ∈ R̄_a\R_a (Def 3.4, p. 11), with ν_j = e′_{j′−1}/e′_{j′} ≥ 2 strictly
   105	(Def 3.1(ii), p. 10: e_i = gcd(e_{i−1}, β_i) < e_{i−1}).
   106	
   107	(c) Not 3.5 (p. 12): κ_{F_j} = κ′/e′_{j′} and π(F_j) = β′_{j′}/κ′ with
   108	e′_{j′} | β′_{j′} (Def 3.1(ii)), so κ_{F_j}π(F_j) = β′_{j′}/e′_{j′} ∈ ℤ and
   109	κ̄_{F_j} ∈ ℤ.
   110	
   111	(d) P_i's branch passes through F_j, so its series coefficient at the
   112	exponent u := π(F_j) is a root of p_{F_j} (Prop 3.1(**), p. 14: pattern
   113	roots = continuation coefficients of the fiber branches). By (a) the root
   114	set contains no 0, so c_u(P_i) ≠ 0. Since F_j = I_{P′}(α′_{j′}), the series
   115	P′ and P_i agree strictly below u (Def 3.3, p. 11: u ≤ O(P_i, P′)), so
   116	they generate the same exponent lattice L below u; u ∉ L because β′_{j′}
   117	is not divisible by e′_{j′−1} (Def 3.1(i)). A nonzero coefficient of P_i
   118	at an exponent outside its running lattice is, by Def 3.1(i), exactly the
   119	next characteristic exponent of P_i: u = α_s(P_i) for some s. Every V_a
   120	vertex on I_{P_i}([0, π(P_i-vertex)]) is enumerated by the descent (Not
   121	3.3, p. 12), every characteristic value of P_i is a V_a vertex (Def 3.4),
   122	and every segment vertex is characteristic by the above — so consecutive
   123	segment vertices sit at consecutive characteristic exponents of P_i. The
   124	edge F_{j−1} → F_j then has parent at α_s(P_i), child at α_{s−1}(P_i):
   125	with P = P_i this is literally case (II) of Prop 9.3 ("F ∈ V_{1,a} and
   126	u = α_{j−1}"), and the case-(I)/(II) equations (a)–(d) apply. ∎
   127	
   128	**Remarks.** (R1) The depth of an M=1 segment below its pole vertex is
   129	therefore at most the number m_i of characteristic exponents of P_i, so
   130	Π_j ν_j ≤ e_0 = κ_i and d ≤ log₂ κ_i. This is finite for every actual
   131	(f,g) but κ_i is NOT bounded by (m, td) — td is the topological degree
   132	(Def 5.1, p. 28), which does not control the Puiseux denominator at
   133	infinity. This is the honest reason a depth CAP (option (a)) is
   134	unavailable and depth-invariance is the right statement. (R2) The
   135	phase-4 "zch chain children" of cases/twopole_check.py (ν_F = 1, p = η^i)
   136	have a single distinct root, hence are NOT V_a vertices (St 3.16 iff) —
   137	engine over-generation, harmless there (they produced no residue and, by
   138	§3 R3 below, they conserve w anyway).
