   209	## 5. DS4 (the jump-cell menu factors through w)
   210	
   211	Let G be a merge with arriving all-μ=1 edges from last-segment vertices
   212	H_e, and let (dp_c, dq_c) be the merged reduced pattern (MP6(d) menu:
   213	IIa (rν, (r+l)ν+1); ZCH ((r−1)ν+1, (r−1+l)ν+1); I (r, r+l)),
   214	Δ_c := dq_c − dp_c.
   215	
   216	### 5a. The handshake
   217	
   218	For every μ_e = 1 edge governed by the case-(I)/(II) equations:
   219	
   220	    κ̄_G = (κ̄_e + n_e)/ν_e,   D_G/i := μ_e(ρ_e + n_e)/ν_e = (ρ_e + n_e)/ν_e
   221	    ⟹  **κ̄_G − D_G/i = (κ̄_e − ρ_e)/ν_e = w_e.**
   222	
   223	One line, cell-independent. Consequences: (i) the join condition (common
   224	(κ̄_G, D/i) across edges, SHEET6-MP-REVIEW §5c layer J) forces **all
   225	arriving chains to carry the same w**; (ii) solving the edge equation
   226	against the cell exactly as in §3 gives
   227	
   228	    κ̄_G = w·dq_c/Δ_c,   D_G/i = w·dp_c/Δ_c,   ρ_G = w/Δ_c,
   229	
   230	so the **entire merged-child datum depends only on (w, cell)** — not on
   231	the segment depth, not on (ν_e, n_e, κ̄_e) separately. (The per-edge
   232	admissibility n_e ∈ ℕ*, n_e ≡ −κ̄_e (mod ν_e) does depend on the state
   233	(w, ν_e, n_e); see §6 for why this does not spoil stabilization.)
   234	
   235	### 5b. Cells are finite per w
   236	
   237	For jump cells (l ≥ 1, MP7) one has dq_c ≤ (r+1)Δ_c in all three families
   238	(elementary: e.g. IIa: (r+1)(lν+1) − ((r+l)ν+1) = r(lν+1−ν) ≥ 0), so
   239	**w < κ̄_G ≤ (r+1)w, κ̄_G ∈ ℤ**. Fixing κ̄_G in this finite window and
   240	setting Di := κ̄_G − w:
   241	
   242	- IIa: ν(wr − Di·l) = Di ⟹ ν determined per l < wr/Di;
   243	- ZCH: ν(l·Di − w(r−1)) = w ⟹ ν determined per l ≤ wr/Di;
   244	- I: l = wr/Di (ν = 1).
   245	
   246	Finitely many (family, ν, l, M = gcd) per (w, r ≤ m) — the SHEET6-MP-REVIEW
   247	§5a per-frame finiteness, now *uniform in depth*. Cascade cells (l = 0,
   248	M = 1 emission) are the infinite ν-family, but their child is again an
   249	M=1 segment entry with **w_child = w·(r+l)/(lν+1)** (= r·w at l = 0),
   250	ν-independent at l = 0; with MP1's Σ(r−1) = m−1 the composed closure
   251	multiplies at most m−1 such factors — still a finite computable w-set.
   252	
   253	### 5c. Correction: the ZCH 0-edge is case (III)
   254	
   255	The 0-direction chain's own pole has coefficient 0 at π(G) (that is what
   256	"chain at the 0-direction" means), so π(G) is NOT a characteristic
   257	exponent of that pole's series, while G ∈ V_{1,a} whenever ν_G ≥ 2
   258	(ν ≥ 2 forces V_1 membership, Not 3.4; π(G) is off-lattice, realized as a
   259	characteristic value of the branch that does jump there). For the 0-edge
   260	Prop 9.3 case (III) applies ("F ∈ V_{1,a} and u > α_{j−1}"), equations
   261	(e)–(h) with ν := ν_F: i-normalized, κ̄_G = κ̄_e + n′, D_G/i = ρ_e + n′
   262	with n′ = n/ν_G ∈ (1/ν_G)ℕ*, hence handshake
   263	
   264	    **κ̄_G − D_G/i = κ̄_e − ρ_e = ν_e·w_e   (0-edge, case III).**
   265	
   266	The join then demands w_other = ν_e·w_{0-chain} with ν_e ≥ 2: **ZCH cells
   267	are reachable only when the arriving chains carry w's in an integer ratio
   268	ν_e ≥ 2**. In particular, at td = 6 (single value w = 2) ZCH is
   269	unreachable outright, and the corrected pre-suffix jump menu is exactly
   270	{IIa (2,3,1), M = 2} — the promoted residue (machine check 7; the engine's
   271	(a)–(d)-modelled ZCH child (M=3, κ̄=3) was suffix-killed in the promoted
   272	record, so no promoted conclusion changes, but the book spec should carry
   273	the corrected ZCH reach equation). The non-0 edges of a ZCH merge, and
   274	all IIa edges, are case (II) (DS1(d) argument at the merge: nonzero
   275	arrival coefficient at an off-lattice exponent = characteristic exponent
   276	of the arriving pole's series); I-family merges (ν_G = 1) are case (I)
   277	(V_{2,a}\V_{1,a}, lattice meet). All non-0 edges keep the §5a handshake.
   278	
   279	### 5d. Root merges: w < 1, and the case-(IV) grounding of the n = ν−κ̄ pin
   280	
   281	(0,y) ∉ V_{1,a} ∪ V_{2,a} (α_j > 0 and O(P,P′) > 0), so a root-merge edge
   282	is Prop 9.3 **case (IV)**, equations (i)–(m) (p. 51): (i) ν_e = κ_e; (j)
   283	(1−v)κ_e < ν_e, i.e. κ̄_e < ν_e; (k) d_{(0,y)} = (D_e + (ν_e − κ̄_e)·
   284	deg p_e)/ν_e. Setting n* := ν_e − κ̄_e ≥ 1, (k) is the (c)-equation with
   285	the edge parameter FORCED: n_e = n* — this derives, on printed ground, the
   286	"n_e = ν_e − κ̄_e, both parents κ̄ < ν" pin that SHEET6-MP-REVIEW §2c
   287	established for MP9's root clause (and (l)–(m) add d < deg p_G and the
   288	M-divisibility as extra filters). The handshake at the root (κ̄_{(0,y)} =
   289	1, St 9.2(iii); D_{(0,y)} = l_f, St 9.2(i); D/i = l_f/i₀):
   290	
   291	    **w_e = 1 − l_f/i₀ ∈ (0,1): root merges require w < 1.**
   292	
   293	At td = 6, W = {2}: root meets are dead in one line — reproving the §2c
   294	raised-caps sweep result exactly (machine check 2: every reachable frame
   295	has w = 2 ≥ 1).
