    For given n, d the set Xd′ := (JAn,d,d′ ×Spec Q) ⊂ JEn,d ×Spec Q is a
constructible set. Therefore, we get an infinite growing chain of constructible
subsets X1 ⊂ X2 ⊂ . . . of the scheme of finite type JEn,d ×Spec Q over Q.
    Let us assume JCn and fix an integer d ≥ 1. Then ∪d′ ≥1 Xd′ = X
where X := JEn,d ×Spec Q. Then it follows from the standard properties
of constructible sets (see [6], Corrollaire 1.9.8, Chapitre IV) that there ex-
ists an integer d′ such that Xd′ = X. Alternatively, one can use a result of
O. Gabber (see [3], Theorem 1.2) which says that for an automorphism f of
k[x1 , . . . , xn ] of degree ≤ d in the above sense (k is a field of any character-
istic), the inverse map has the degree ≤ dn−1 . Hence one can a priori set
d′ = dn−1 . Anyhow, the Jacobian conjecture can be rephrased as the equality
JAn,d,d′ (C) = JEn,d (C).
    The following statement is obvious.
Lemma 1 Let φ : A → B be an immersion of schemes of finite type over
Z. Then φ induces a bijection between A(C) and B(C) if and only if there
exists a finite set of primes S such that φ induces a bijection between A(k)
and B(k) for any field k with char k ∈/ S ∪ {0}.
                                       (J)
   We apply it to the projection prn,d,d′ . The conclusion is that the Jacobian
conjecture JCn is equivalent to the following
Conjecture 1 (JCn in finite characteristic) For any d ≥ 1 there exists d′ ≥
1 and a finite set of primes S such that for any field k with char k ∈
                                                                     / S ∪ {0}
                                n      n
and any polynomial map φ : Ak → Ak of degree ≤ d with Jacobian 1, the
inverse map exists and has degree ≤ d′ .
   The equivalence of JCn and the above conjecture in finite characteristic
was first established by K. Adjamagbo in [1].


3     More about Weyl algebras
3.1    Weyl algebras over an arbitrary base
One can define an algebra An,R for arbitrary commutative ring R exactly in
the same way as for fields of characteristic zero. This algebra is free as a R-

                                             4
module. It has a canonical basis consisting of monomials (y1α1 . . . y2n α2n
                                                                             )(α1 ,...,α2n )∈Z2n
                                                                                              ≥0
                                                                                                 .
                                                                      n
Although for any R the algebra An,R maps to the algebra D(AR ) of differen-
tial operators acting on R[x1 , . . . , xn ], these two algebras are not isomorphic
in general. For example, if R is an algebra over Z/pZ for some prime p, then
the operator (d/dx1 )p is zero.
    We say that an endomorphism f of the algebra An,R has degree ≤ d if the
image f (yi) of any generator yi ∈ An,R , i = 1, . . . , 2n is a linear combination
with coefficients in R of the monomials of degree ≤ d. In a manner completely
parallel to the previous section, we can define schemes of finite type DEn,d ,
                                   (D)
DAn,d,d′ , and the projection prn,d,d′ . Also, we can make a reformulation of
the Dixmier conjecture in the same way as for the Jacobian conjecture.

3.2     The Weyl algebra in finite characteristic as an Azu-
        maya algebra
It is a classical fact that in finite characteristic the algebra An,R has a big
center, and it is moreover an Azumaya algebra of its center (see [8]).
    We will use the following slightly non-standard definition of an Azumaya
algebra (see e.g. [7], Proposition 2.1, Chapter IV):
Definition 1 For a commutative ring R an Azumaya algebra over R of rank
N ≥ 1 is an associative unital algebra A over R which is a finitely generated
R-module and such that there exists a finitely generated faithfully flat exten-
sion R′ ⊂ R of R such that the pullback algebra A′ := A ⊗R R′ is isomorphic
to the matrix algebra
                     Mat(N ⊗ N, R′ ) = Mat(N × N, Z) ⊗ R′
as an algebra over R′ .
    It follows by descent that the center of an Azumaya algebra over R co-
incides with R. Also, an Azumaya algebra A considered as a R-module is
a finitely generated projective module, in other words, a vector bundle over
Spec R. This bundle has rank N 2 , its fibers are associative algebras, and the
fiber over any point of Spec R over an algebraically closed field k is isomorphic
to the matrix algebra Mat(N × N, k).
Proposition 1 For any commutative algebra R over Z/pZ where p is a
prime, the algebra An,R is an Azumaya algebra of rank pn over R[x1 , . . . , x2n ].
The central element of An,R corresponding to variable xi is yip .

