of the degrees of polynomials fvcentr (xi ). The degree of endomorphism fv is
defined as the maximum over i ∈ {1, . . . , 2n} of the degrees (in the sense
of Bernstein filtration, by the degree of monomials in the standard basis of
An,k ) of elements fv (yi). We claim that for each index i both degrees coincide
with each other. The reason is the following. Let di be the degree of fv (yi ).
We claim that the degree of fv (yip ) = (fv (yi ))p considered as an element of
An,k , is equal to pdi . It follows from the following

Lemma 7 The degree is an additive character of the multiplicative monoid
of non-zero elements in An,k .

Proof: It follows immediately from the consideration of Bernstein filtration
on An,k and the remark that the product of non-zero homogeneous polyno-
mials is a non-zero polynomial. 2
    The degree of fv (yip ) considered as an element of Center(An,k ) is 1/p times
its degree in An,k , i.e. it is equal to di . Proposition 3 is proven. 2
