The primary-source check changes the lane’s framing. There are two different p-curvatures here: the inverse-Jacobian connection naturally attached to a Keller map has identically zero p-curvature, while Tsuchimoto’s nonzero p-curvature lives on a splitting module for the characteristic-\(p\) Weyl algebra and starts from a Weyl endomorphism. BKK’s proof uses the same Weyl-center shadow through its canonical Poisson bracket. Conflating these two objects is exactly what makes row 20 look more promising than it is.
Primary sources establish that Tsuchimoto’s connection belongs to the Azumaya splitting of a Weyl algebra, whereas a Keller map’s inverse-Jacobian pushforward connection is a separate zero-p-curvature object. BKK’s center/Poisson construction confirms the direction Weyl endomorphism → symplectic Keller map, not the converse.
