+# Reduction mod (p) / (p)-curvature: the residue-A lane
+
+**Date:** 2026-08-23  
+**Charge:** execute `APPROACHES.md` row 20 as a standalone lane; set up the
+(p)-curvature attached to a plane Keller map, test for a residue-A necessary
+condition, and identify the exact arrow to the Dixmier side.  
+**Verdict:** **KNOWN-HOLLOW as a new residue-A obstruction; REAL as a precise
+dictionary and scope correction.**
+
+The central finding is a distinction between two objects that should not be
+called “the (p)-curvature of the Keller map” interchangeably.
+
+1. A plane Keller map has a canonical inverse-Jacobian connection on its
+   direct image. Its (p)-curvature is **identically zero for every Keller
+   map in characteristic (p)**. This gives a necessary condition, but it is
+   a formal consequence of (J(P,Q)=1), hence adds no equation to residue-A.
+2. Tsuchimoto's useful (p)-curvature is on a splitting module for the
+   characteristic-(p) Weyl algebra. It starts with a **Weyl-algebra
+   endomorphism**, and proves that its map on the (p)-center is symplectic.
+   Belov-Kanel--Kontsevich (BKK) obtain the same quantum-to-classical arrow
+   from the canonical Poisson bracket on the center. Neither construction
+   sends an arbitrary commutative Keller map, much less a finite residue-A
+   jet, to an (A_1) endomorphism.
+
+Thus the repository's modular nonemptiness does not cross to the DC(1) lane.
+A full plane Keller map would give a special endomorphism of (A_2) by
+pullback of differential operators, but that is the familiar
+(mathrm{DC}_2\Rightarrowmathrm{JC}_2) direction and supplies no new
+constraint before the template has been algebraized.
+
+---
+
+## 1. The connection attached to a plane Keller map
+
+### 1.1 Setup
+
+Let (k) be a perfect field of characteristic (p>0), and let
+
+\[
+ F=(P,Q):\mathbb A^2_{x,y}\longrightarrow\mathbb A^2_{u,v},
+ \qquad P,Q\in B:=k[x,y],
+\]
+
+with
+
+\[
+ J:=P_xQ_y-P_yQ_x=1.
+\]
+
+Put (A=k[u,v]) and make (B) an (A)-algebra by (u\mapsto P),
+(v\mapsto Q). The inverse Jacobian gives the unique lifts of the target
+coordinate derivations:
+
+\[
+ \delta_u=Q_y\partial_x-Q_x\partial_y,
+ \qquad
+ \delta_v=-P_y\partial_x+P_x\partial_y.                 \tag{1.1}
+\]
+
+They satisfy
+
+\[
+\begin{array}{c|cc}
+ &P&Q\\ \hline
+\delta_u&1&0\\
+\delta_v&0&1
+\end{array}
+\]
+
+and commute. Indeed, ([\delta_u,\delta_v]) kills both (P) and (Q),
+and a derivation of (B) killing both is zero because (dP,dQ) are a
+(B)-basis of (Omega^1_{B/k}).
+
+On the largest target open set (U) over which (F) is finite etale,
+(E=(F_*\mathcal O_{mathbb A^2})|_U) is a vector bundle of rank equal to
+the generic degree. It carries the integrable connection
+
+\[
+ \nabla b=du\otimes\delta_u(b)+dv\otimes\delta_v(b).      \tag{1.2}
+\]
+
+The same formula defines an (A)-connection on the quasi-coherent module
+(B) without assuming global finiteness. Equation (1.2), not a rank-one
+exponential connection and not Tsuchimoto's splitting connection, is the
+canonical connection directly attached to (F).
+
+### 1.2 Its (p)-curvature
+
+For (D\in\operatorname{Der}_k(A)), write (D^{[p]}) for its restricted
+(p)-th power. The (p)-curvature is
+
+\[
+ \psi_\nabla(D)=\nabla_D^p-\nabla_{D^{[p]}}
+ \in\operatorname{End}_A(E).                              \tag{1.3}
+\]
+
+Since (partial_u^{[p]}=partial_v^{[p]}=0),
+
+\[
+ \psi_\nabla(\partial_u)=\delta_u^p,
+ \qquad
+ \psi_\nabla(\partial_v)=\delta_v^p.                      \tag{1.4}
+\]
+
+Here the powers on the right are iterates acting on (B). In characteristic
+(p), the (p)-th iterate of a derivation is again a derivation. Moreover,
+
+\[
+ \delta_u^p(P)=\delta_u^{p-1}(1)=0,
+ \quad \delta_u^p(Q)=0,
+\]
+
+and similarly for (delta_v^p). Because a derivation of (B) is determined
+by its values on (P,Q), both iterates vanish. Hence:
+
+> **Proposition 1 (automatic zero (p)-curvature).** For every plane Keller
+> map over (k), the inverse-Jacobian connection (1.2) is flat and
+> (psi_\nabla=0).
+
+Equivalently, over function fields (k(P,Q)\subset k(x,y)), the extension is
+finite separable and (delta_u^p,delta_v^p) are derivations over
+(k(P,Q)); such derivations of a finite separable extension are zero. This
+also shows that the conclusion is not sensitive to the behavior at the
+nonproperness locus.
+
+Cartier descent interprets zero (p)-curvature as Frobenius descent of the
+connection. It does **not** interpret it as degree one, global properness, or
+polynomial invertibility.
+
+### 1.3 A decisive negative control
+
+The condition cannot distinguish automorphisms even inside finite etale
+maps. For every (p),
+
+\[
+ F_{\mathrm{AS}}=(x-x^p,y)
+\]
+
+has Jacobian one and is finite etale of degree (p), hence is not a
+polynomial automorphism. Its lifted derivations are simply
+(delta_u=\partial_x,delta_v=\partial_y), so its (p)-curvature is zero.
+This Artin--Schreier example is a complete negative control against using
+(psi_\nabla=0) as an invertibility screen.
+
+---
+
+## 2. Where Tsuchimoto's (p)-curvature actually lives
+
+### 2.1 The characteristic-(p) Weyl algebra
+
+Let (W_n(k)) be generated by (X_i,D_i), with
+([D_i,X_j]=\delta_{ij}). In characteristic (p),
+
+\[
+ Z(W_n)=k[X_1^p,\ldots,X_n^p,D_1^p,\ldots,D_n^p],          \tag{2.1}
+\]
+
+and (W_n) is Azumaya of rank (p^n) over this center. After adjoining
+formal (p)-th roots of the central coordinates, the Azumaya algebra splits:
+
+\[
+ W_n\otimes_Z Z^{1/p}\simeq
+ \operatorname{End}_{Z^{1/p}}(\mathcal E),
+ \qquad \operatorname{rank}\mathcal E=p^n.                \tag{2.2}
+\]
+
+Tsuchimoto constructs a flat connection on
+(operatorname{End}(\mathcal E)) making the original Weyl algebra
+horizontal, lifts it to a projectively flat connection on (mathcal E),
+and recovers the standard symplectic form as its scalar curvature. Because
+that lifted connection is not flat, he modifies it to a flat connection and
+computes the modified connection's (p)-curvature. Comparing this object
+with its pullback under a Weyl endomorphism gives the gauge equation in his
+Proposition 3.2. For degree bounded independently of (p), the scalar error
+vanishes for sufficiently large (p), so the induced map on the root of the
+center preserves the symplectic form (Corollary 3.3). An ultraproduct then
+returns to characteristic zero (Proposition 7.1 and Corollary 7.3).
+
+The logical input is therefore
+
+\[
+ \boxed{\text{Weyl endomorphism}}
+ \longrightarrow
+ \boxed{\text{pullback of the Azumaya splitting connection}}
+ \longrightarrow
+ \boxed{\text{symplectic map on }\operatorname{Spec}Z^{1/p}}. \tag{2.3}
+\]
+
+It is not
+
+\[
+ \text{arbitrary Keller map}\longrightarrow\text{Weyl endomorphism}. \tag{2.4}
+\]
+
+This direction is explicit in [Tsuchimoto 2005, §§2--3 and §7](https://doi.org/10.18910/7472).
+
+### 2.2 The BKK version
+
+BKK avoid the splitting-connection calculation. Spread a characteristic-zero
+Weyl endomorphism out over a finitely generated integral ring and reduce
+modulo (p). The reduction preserves the large center. On that center they
+define
+
+\[
+ \{a,b\}=\frac{[\widetilde a,\widetilde b]}{p}\pmod p,     \tag{2.5}
+\]
+
+where the tildes are lifts before reduction. In the coordinates (2.1), this
+is the standard symplectic Poisson bracket. A Weyl endomorphism preserves
+(2.5), hence its center map is a (2n)-variable symplectic Keller map.
+Assuming (mathrm{JC}_{2n}), that center map is invertible for all sufficiently
+large (p); the equal-rank Azumaya map is then invertible, and spreading out
+returns an inverse in characteristic zero. This is Theorem 1 of
+[Belov-Kanel--Kontsevich](https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0512171):
+
+\[
+ \mathrm{JC}_{2n}\Longrightarrow\mathrm{DC}_n.             \tag{2.6}
+\]
+
+Tsuchimoto's (p)-curvature and the BKK Poisson center are two realizations
+of the same **quantum-to-classical** shadow. BKK do not require a connection
+attached to a pre-existing commutative Keller map.
+
+### 2.3 Why “zero above, nonzero center below” is not a contradiction
+
+The abstract crystalline/Weyl differential operator (D^p) is not the same
+as the (p)-fold action of (D) on the polynomial module. In particular,
+(partial_x^p) is a nonzero central element of the Weyl algebra but acts as
+zero on (k[x,y]).
+
+For a vector field (D=a\partial_x+b\partial_y), the central
+(p)-curvature element is
+
+\[
+ D^p-D^{[p]}=a^p\partial_x^p+b^p\partial_y^p.              \tag{2.7}
+\]
+
+For (D=\delta_u,delta_v), the restricted vector-field powers
+(D^{[p]}) vanish by Proposition 1, while the central elements (2.7) need
+not vanish in the Weyl algebra. The direct-image connection sees the zero
+cotangent central character; Tsuchimoto's splitting family sees all central
+characters. This is the precise relation between the two uses of
+(p)-curvature.
+
+---
+
+## 3. The full Keller-to-(A_2) lift and its center shadow
+
+Although there is no automatic (A_1) quantization, a full plane Keller map
+does canonically pull back differential operators in **two** variables. With
+target Weyl generators (u,v,\partial_u,\partial_v), define
+
+\[
+\begin{aligned}
+ \Phi_F(u)&=P, & \Phi_F(v)&=Q,\\
+ \Phi_F(\partial_u)&=Q_y\partial_x-Q_x\partial_y,
+ &\Phi_F(\partial_v)&=-P_y\partial_x+P_x\partial_y.
+\end{aligned}                                                \tag{3.1}
+\]
+
+Equations (1.1) and flatness give all six CCRs, so
+(Phi_F:A_2\to A_2) is a Weyl endomorphism. This is the standard proof of
+(mathrm{DC}_2\Rightarrowmathrm{JC}_2): if (F) were a nonautomorphic
+Keller map, (Phi_F) would be a non-surjective (A_2) endomorphism.
+
+In characteristic (p), apply (2.7) and take (p)-th-root center
+coordinates. If ((x,y;\alpha,\beta)) are the source cotangent coordinates,
+the center shadow of (3.1) is
+
+\[
+ \widetilde F(x,y;\alpha,\beta)=
+ \bigl(P,Q;
+ Q_y\alpha-Q_x\beta,
+ -P_y\alpha+P_x\beta\bigr).                 \tag{3.2}
+\]
+
+This is the inverse-cotangent lift of (F). It is automatically exact
+symplectic, since
+
+\[
+ \widetilde F^*(\alpha_u,du+\beta_v,dv)
+ =\alpha,dx+\beta,dy.                                      \tag{3.3}
+\]
+
+Thus the (4)-variable BKK Keller condition on this special Weyl
+endomorphism is another algebraic consequence of (J(P,Q)=1). It does not
+cut the plane Keller locus further.
+
+This gives the exact dictionary relevant to the repository:
+
+\[
+\begin{array}{ccccc}
+\text{full plane Keller }F
+&\longrightarrow&
+\Phi_F\in\operatorname{End}(A_2)
+&\longrightarrow&
+\widetilde F:T^*\mathbb A^2\to T^*\mathbb A^2,\\[2mm]
+\text{finite residue-A jet}
+&\not\longrightarrow&
+\text{polynomial }A_2\text{ endomorphism}
+&\not\longrightarrow&
+\text{global center map}.
+\end{array}                                                   \tag{3.4}
+\]
+
+The first line is real but conditional on having a full polynomial pair.
+The second line is the current state of the campaign.
+
+---
+
+## 4. Necessary-condition audit on residue-A
+
+### 4.1 What is actually banked
+
+The phrase “three-prime modular nonemptiness” needs a scope qualifier.
+
+| object | banked modular result | primes | what is not present |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| D23 `a00pp` det23/row22red fiber | proper 509-element GB, dimension 11 over (overline{\mathbf F}_p) | 105337, 105673, 200257 | global (P,Q); a third-prime rational witness |
+| D23 full 36-fiber atlas | 36/36 nonempty, dimension 11 | 105337, 105673 | third-prime atlas; global (P,Q) |
+| D25 family | 576 copies of (mathbb A^{14}), hence nonempty | 105337, 105673 | third-prime result; formal germ; global (P,Q) |
+
+The sources are [SHEET6-DIRECTIONB §§8.S5--8.S8 and 9.S2](../SHEET6-DIRECTIONB.md),
+the [D23 witness banks](../cases/d23_witnesses_p105337.json), and the
+[D25 replay](../cases/d25_certificate_replay.json). The D23 witness files
+exist only for 105337 and 105673; at 200257 the variety result is over the
+algebraic closure and the recorded (W^4) branch has no
+(mathbf F_p)-rational chart point. The D25 replay explicitly scopes itself
+to the first two primes.
+
+All these objects are finite-window, chart-local systems: residue-A,
+B-frozen, no-log, `PIN42`, (W_1W_2\ne0), with an emission-fidelity caveat.
+Their variables are tail, radical, pole-scale, and compatibility
+coordinates. An inspection of the three det23 headers gives 22 such
+coordinates; there is no coefficient list for global polynomials (P,Q),
+no finite etale (A)-algebra (B), and no Weyl splitting module or gauge
+matrix.
+
+### 4.2 Candidate gate N: zero (p)-curvature
+
+**Gate:** require
+
+\[
+ \delta_u^p=\delta_v^p=0.                                   \tag{N}
+\]
+
+**Result:** **PASS IDENTICALLY / ZERO DISCRIMINATION.** Proposition 1 proves
+(N) in the coordinate ring of every exact Keller pair, before any residue-A
+specialization. It is already in the differential ideal generated by the
+exact Jacobian relation in the strongest possible sense: no residue-A
